 &nbsp; 
 
 We don't know why Nvidia's board fired Jensen Huang two weeks ago. We also don't know why they rehired him yesterday.

 All of the celebration and condemnation are educated guesses at best and ignorant noise at worst. If the company and its CEO are really too big to fail — linchpins to America's economy and tech sovereignty — then the American people deserve answers as to what actually happened.

 Nvidia's board and Jensen himself should stop hiding behind PR pablum and share facts. Even if they are uncomfortable. Was he fired because: He was sleeping with an alleged spy? He was sleeping with a major customer? He was sleeping with a direct report? Other employees too? He lied to the board? He shared sensitive information with someone he shouldn't? He sidestepped U.S. export controls? He was at serious risk of prosecution? Fears of investor lawsuits?

 Was he reinstated because: She definitely wasn't a spy? President Trump promised that Jensen wouldn't be prosecuted, or that he'd get a preemptive pardon? Other political pressures? The value destruction was too great? Board members were being ostracized? Employees adored him? Fears of investor lawsuits?

 There have been reports and speculation about all of the above, but we don't actually know any of it. The resignation and reinstatement announcements were bereft of such details, despite the trillions of dollars that moved on these choices. The bottom line: There is bipartisan fervor in Congress for investigations, and Nvidia claims that its own independent inquiry is ongoing.

 The former risks becoming a political circus, and the latter at this point feels pro forma. Eleven people have definitive answers. It's time for them to share. 
 
