 &nbsp; 
 
 
 The BFD 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Annelise Capossela/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Amazon (Nasdaq: AMZN) has agreed to buy the chip optimization software assets of Decart , an Israeli developer of world models, for a reported $7.5 billion.

 Decart's founding team and a group of its engineers will join Amazon, which is not purchasing Decart's world models. Why it's the BFD: Big Tech keeps seeking to sidestep antitrust scrutiny by structuring multibillion-dollar AI deals as asset sales instead of as corporate acquisitions.

 Catch up quick: Decart has raised over $450m in VC funding from firms like Radical Ventures, Benchmark, Sequoia Capital, Aleph VC, and Zeev Ventures. 

 It previously held sale talks with Anthropic (Nasdaq: ANT). The bottom line: " Amazon, which has so far been using its Trainium chips at its own data centers, is preparing to start selling them to customers as a competitor to Nvidia. Decart's technology could make it easier for customers to run their workloads on Amazon chips, rather than Nvidia's, which has been the gold standard so far." — Rebecca Bellan, TechCrunch 

 
 
